# Vaultwarden stack secrets (services VM)
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/vaultwarden.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys —
# the stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# 🚨 THIS IS THE FIRST FILE IN THE REPO WITH A `$`-HEAVY VALUE, AND THE RULE
# IS NOT OPTIONAL. Read the ADMIN_TOKEN section before editing anything here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_TOKEN — the /admin panel credential.
#
# Generate:
#   docker run --rm vaultwarden/server:1.37.2 /vaultwarden hash
# and paste the resulting `$argon2id$...` string BELOW, IN SINGLE QUOTES.
#
# 🚨 SINGLE QUOTES ARE REQUIRED. Docker Compose interpolates values read from
# env_file, so a BARE argon2 PHC has `$argon2id`, `$v` and `$m` expanded to
# nothing and the container receives a mangled, shorter string. Verified
# empirically (finding #30): the same value written three ways arrives as
#
#   SQ='$argon2id$v=19$m=65540,...'  ->  $argon2id$v=19$m=65540,...   ✅
#   BARE=$argon2id$v=19$m=65540,...  ->  =19=65540,...                🚨
#   DQ="$argon2id$v=19$m=65540"      ->  =19=65540                    🚨
#
# The mangled forms do not error. Vaultwarden's admin handler dispatches on a
# literal `$argon2` prefix test, so a mangled value falls through to a
# PLAINTEXT COMPARE of whatever you type against that mangled string. /admin
# then rejects everything, and the only clue is a startup line reading
# "[NOTICE] You are using a plain text ADMIN_TOKEN which is insecure" — which
# is accurate, misleading, and scrolls past on first boot.
#
# ⚠ Do NOT `$$`-escape it. That advice in upstream's .env.template is for an
# inline compose `environment:` block; this stack uses env_file, where `$$`
# would arrive as a literal doubled dollar.
#
# Two OTHER failure modes, for completeness — both are loud, unlike the above:
#   - a malformed PHC that still starts with `$argon2` is a HARD STARTUP
#     FAILURE (validate_config errors and the container never comes up). This
#     is the good one.
#   - an empty value starts normally with /admin DISABLED and prints a
#     [WARNING] saying so.
#
# Rotating it is safe and cheap; existing admin sessions survive.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_TOKEN='changeme_argon2id_phc_in_single_quotes'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OIDC client credentials — LEAVE EMPTY until the Authentik side exists.
#
# Empty is a complete state, not a placeholder: SSO_ENABLED is unset in
# compose.yaml, so Vaultwarden authenticates with its own master-password
# accounts. Turning SSO on later needs SSO_ENABLED=true and
# SSO_AUTHORITY=https://auth.idanreed.com/application/o/vaultwarden alongside
# these two.
#
# The Authentik provider needs exactly ONE redirect URI:
#   https://vaultwarden.svc.idanreed.com/identity/connect/oidc-signin
# ⚠ Do NOT also register `bitwarden://sso-callback`. Vaultwarden hardcodes
# per-client redirects, but they never reach the IdP — they only tell
# Vaultwarden where to bounce the client app at the end of the flow. Chasing
# that scheme when mobile SSO fails is a dead end.
#
# Note SSO does NOT replace the master password: the vault is end-to-end
# encrypted and Authentik only gates the session. That is also why forward
# auth is NOT used in front of this vhost — see the Caddyfile.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SSO_CLIENT_ID=
SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIRST ACCOUNT — an operator step, and deliberately not automated.
#
# There is no headless path and there should not be one. Bitwarden
# registration is CLIENT-SIDE CRYPTOGRAPHY: the client derives a master key,
# generates a random symmetric key wrapped under an HKDF-stretched master key,
# and generates an RSA keypair wrapped the same way. An init container could
# reimplement it — and if any step were subtly wrong the server would accept
# the registration and produce a vault that NO REAL CLIENT CAN EVER OPEN. That
# failure surfaces the first time you actually need a password.
#
# compose.yaml ships SIGNUPS_ALLOWED=false. Two ways in:
#   (a) PREFERRED — log into https://vaultwarden.svc.idanreed.com/admin with
#       the ADMIN_TOKEN above and invite yourself. With no SMTP configured the
#       panel shows the invite link to copy. Registration by invite works with
#       signups closed, so the instance is never open, not even briefly.
#   (b) Flip SIGNUPS_ALLOWED=true in compose.yaml, register once from the web
#       vault, flip it back and redeploy. Simpler, but there is a window in
#       which anyone who can reach the tailnet can create an account — and
#       "anyone on the tailnet" includes every device you have ever enrolled.
#
# Do NOT use SIGNUPS_DOMAINS_WHITELIST as a substitute for closing signups: it
# still leaves registration open to anyone who can reach the port.
#
# ⚠ RESTIC_PASSWORD MUST NOT LIVE ONLY IN HERE. Vaultwarden's own backup is
# encrypted with it, so a vault that holds the only copy is a vault you cannot
# restore. stacks/backrest/.sops.env.example says the same thing from the
# other side.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
